=begin
  guidebookquest.lic

  ATTEMPTS to automate the Ebon Gate Festival "Welcome to Evermore Hollow" quest.
	
	I'm not making new characters to test this for days.  It mostly works.

  Commands:
    ;guidebookquest   - Start (or resume) the guidebook tour. Picks up
                         wherever QUEST INFO/the guidebook's own chapter
                         text says you left off.

  Requires the "Welcome to Evermore Hollow" quest to already be started
  (QUEST INFO shows "You are currently undertaking this quest.") and a
  shadowy leatherbound guidebook on hand or in an open container.

  Author: Claude Code(Creator and Feature Additions), Codex(Bug Fixes and Review), RuseofFools (Very Little)
  Required: Lich >= 5.20.0
  Tags: eg, ebon gate, ebon, guidebook, guide, quest, evermore hollow
  Version: 1.47.0

  Recently changed:
    v1.47.0  (2026-10-11)
      - Fixed the quest-complete exit not firing reliably: confirmed
        live the script kept going past "You have completed Welcome to
        Evermore Hollow!" all the way through another chapter's own
        completion (Chapter 5, in the transcript that caught this) and
        logged ITS quest info instead of stopping. Root cause:
        Chapter 13's own "get"/"read" fputs right after "quest complete"
        can advance the guidebook into a new chapter's own "You
        completed this chapter." before the main dispatch loop ever
        gets around to processing the quest-complete confirmation line
        itself -- the CHAPTER 13 COMPLETED branch's exit was only ever
        reached if that line got dispatched before anything else
        overtook it, which isn't guaranteed. Now captures the response
        to "quest complete" directly and exits immediately from inside
        Chapter 13's own code instead of hoping the confirmation surfaces
        later through the normal line-by-line dispatch.
=end

# Resolves a room target for go2: a bare Integer/numeric String is treated
# as a Lich internal room id (LN) and converted to "u<uid>" via Room[].uid;
# anything else (an existing "uNNNN" string, a tag like "town"/"exit") is
# passed through unchanged. Falls back to the bare LN (with a warning) if
# the room isn't in the local map database -- go2 may not path correctly
# from an LN in that case, but it's better than silently going nowhere.
def go2_room(target)
  return target.to_s unless target.to_s =~ /\A\d+\z/

  ln = target.to_i
  room = Room[ln]
  uid = room && Array(room.uid).first
  return "u#{uid}" if uid

  echo "guidebookquest: no uid mapped for room #{ln} (not in the local map database) -- falling back to the Lich room id, go2 may not path correctly."
  ln.to_s
end

QUEST_NAME = 'Welcome to Evermore Hollow'

# QUEST/QUEST INFO tracking, ported from intotherift.lic's own pattern:
# the guidebook's own chapter flavor text is what actually drives this
# script (it's reliably specific per chapter), but the game's own QUEST
# INFO objective line is the authoritative ground truth for what the
# quest thinks you should be doing right now -- worth surfacing
# alongside the guidebook text rather than trusting the guidebook's
# wording alone, especially since the two don't always say the same
# thing (confirmed live: QUEST INFO said "Visit the bellhop at the
# Evermore Hotel and ask if he has any quests for you." right after
# Chapter 3 completed, which doesn't resemble any guidebook chapter text
# at all). Lich::Util.issue_command is used instead of a hand-rolled
# DownstreamHook capture since it's the same proven utility
# intotherift.lic relies on for this exact purpose.
# Confirmed live via the raw XML stream: bare "quest" shows generic
# command usage/help (not the table at all -- "Usage: QUEST {option}
# {args}" with LIST/ACCEPT/INFO/etc. listed as options), and intotherift
# .lic's own bare "quest" only ever "works" for that script by accident
# (its quest defaults to slot 1 when the row lookup fails, and "Into the
# Rift" happens to always BE slot 1 for that character, masking the same
# bug). "Welcome to Evermore Hollow" is not reliably any particular
# slot, so that fallback would silently pull the wrong quest's info for
# us. The actual confirmed-working command is "quest list all".
#
# usexml: false is used throughout -- confirmed live that the default
# (usexml: true) capture comes back as just the trailing prompt marker
# ("&gt;") with none of the real multi-line text, almost certainly
# because the response's XML tags (<pushBold/>, <output .../>, etc.)
# don't line up cleanly with issue_command's line-by-line start/end
# pattern matching. The plain-text downstream Lich provides via
# usexml: false doesn't have that problem.
def guidebookquest_quest_row
  lines = Lich::Util.issue_command('quest list all', /Quest Name|Realm|available|not currently/i,
                                    /<prompt/, timeout: 6, silent: true, quiet: true, usexml: false) rescue []
  Array(lines).map(&:to_s)
              .find { |l| l =~ /\d+\)\s+#{Regexp.escape(QUEST_NAME)}/i }
end

def guidebookquest_quest_slot
  @guidebookquest_slot ||= begin
    row = guidebookquest_quest_row
    (row && row[/(\d+)\)/, 1]) ? row[/(\d+)\)/, 1].to_i : nil
  end
end

def guidebookquest_quest_info_text
  slot = guidebookquest_quest_slot
  return '' unless slot

  lines = Lich::Util.issue_command("quest info #{slot}", /Name:|You are|not currently|no active/i,
                                    /<prompt/, timeout: 6, silent: true, quiet: true, usexml: false) rescue []
  Array(lines).map(&:to_s).join("\n")
end

# The real objective is specifically the first non-blank line after
# "You are currently undertaking this quest." -- NOT just "the last
# non-blank line of the captured text". Confirmed live that capture can
# include ambient game output that happened to arrive in the same
# window as the "quest info" response (chat spam, NPC/spirit flavor
# text, other players' messages, etc.), and if any of that lands after
# the real objective line but before the end-of-response prompt, taking
# the last line grabbed the ambient text instead ("Vapor churns on the
# air as the vaporous spirit sinks into itself...", "[Merchant]
# Archales: ..." -- neither of which is an actual quest objective).
def guidebookquest_quest_objective(text)
  match = text.match(/You are currently undertaking this quest\.\s*\n+\s*(.+?)\s*(?:\n|\z)/)
  return match[1].strip if match

  text.lines.map(&:strip).reject(&:empty?).last.to_s
end

# Called after every "CHAPTER N COMPLETED" guidebook transition -- echoes
# the live QUEST INFO objective so progress can be cross-checked against
# the game's own tracking instead of the guidebook text alone.
def guidebookquest_log_quest_info
  # Confirmed live: this installs its own capture hook for
  # "quest info #{slot}", and that hook swallows whatever's still in
  # flight when it goes up -- not just its own response, but any trailing
  # game output arriving in the same window. Calling this BEFORE
  # "flip"/"read my guidebook" (every "CHAPTER N COMPLETED" branch does
  # now) keeps that hook's window clear of the next chapter's text, which
  # used to get vacuumed into it and never reach the main dispatch loop,
  # freezing the script until the guidebook was read again by hand. The
  # sleep here is just a safety margin for the current chapter's own
  # trailing confirmation text ("You completed this chapter.") to land
  # first.
  sleep 1
  text = guidebookquest_quest_info_text
  return if text.empty?

  echo "guidebookquest: QUEST INFO -- #{guidebookquest_quest_objective(text)}"
end

# Chapter 8's debris piles pull a random item from this pool (confirmed
# live) -- several pairs share a noun ("dirty dice" vs "hollow dice",
# "purple bottle" vs "fuzzy bottle", "purple bottle" vs "purple plate"),
# so GET/PUT commands always use the full two-word descriptor, never just
# the trailing noun, to stay unambiguous if a colliding pair both turn up
# in the same run.
# Confirmed live; not necessarily exhaustive -- a "fractured cup" showed
# up that isn't in the set this was first built from, so new pile item
# names may still turn up and need adding here.
MOORLYN_PILE_ITEM_POOL = [
  "red ewer", "dirty dice", "fractured file", "damaged monocle", "black flask",
  "mildewed knife", "mildewed lockpick", "hollow lever", "purple bottle",
  "hollow dice", "fuzzy bottle", "hollow urn", "deformed vial",
  "crystal fishhook", "purple plate", "fractured cup"
].freeze

# Confirmed live: this exact combination balances the scale ("It is
# positioned perfectly on the 'BALANCED' indicator."). Not necessarily
# the ONLY combination that works -- the pile pool is a random subset
# each run, so this specific set of five won't always be available --
# but when it is, placing exactly these skips both the rest of the pile
# search and the backtracking search entirely.
MOORLYN_SCALE_SOLUTION = ["red ewer", "dirty dice", "fractured file", "black flask", "mildewed knife"].freeze

CONTAINER_NOUN_REGEX = /pack|bag|pouch|case|sack|satchel|box|chest|coffer|robe|mandelion/i.freeze

# Captures the full multi-line response to a command (e.g. "look in my
# pack") instead of just one line -- dothistimeout only ever returns the
# single line matching its own pattern, which isn't enough for a
# container listing.
def guidebookquest_capture_block(command, timeout: 5)
  lines = []
  done = false
  hook_name = "guidebookquest_capture_#{Time.now.to_f}"
  DownstreamHook.add(hook_name, proc { |raw|
    done = true if raw =~ /<prompt/
    lines << raw
    raw
  })
  fput command
  deadline = Time.now + timeout
  sleep 0.1 until done || Time.now > deadline
  DownstreamHook.remove(hook_name)
  lines.join("\n")
rescue StandardError
  DownstreamHook.remove(hook_name) rescue nil
  ""
end

# Resume safety: if the script got interrupted and restarted mid-Chapter-8,
# whatever pile items are already being carried are recovered here instead
# of being silently forgotten -- hands, plus actually looking inside every
# worn/held container (GameObj alone only sees a stowed container's own
# name, not its contents, until something looks inside it).
def guidebookquest_held_pile_items
  found = []

  [GameObj.right_hand, GameObj.left_hand].compact.each do |item|
    MOORLYN_PILE_ITEM_POOL.each { |descriptor| found << descriptor if item.name.to_s.include?(descriptor) }
  end

  Array(GameObj.inv).each do |container|
    next unless container.respond_to?(:noun) && container.noun.to_s =~ CONTAINER_NOUN_REGEX

    text = guidebookquest_capture_block("look in my #{container.noun}")
    MOORLYN_PILE_ITEM_POOL.each do |descriptor|
      found << descriptor if text.include?(descriptor)
    end
  end

  found.uniq
end

# Once the overall quest is complete, the Moorlyn pile items are junk --
# confirmed live, just GETting one each produces its own disintegration
# message ("Since there's no reason to hold onto the lever any longer, it
# disintegrates into a fine dust."), no drop/stow needed afterward.
def guidebookquest_discard_pile_items
  items = guidebookquest_held_pile_items
  return if items.empty?

  items.each do |item|
    fput "get #{item}"
    sleep 0.3
  end
  echo "guidebookquest: discarded #{items.size} leftover Moorlyn pile item(s) now that the quest is complete."
end

# Chapter 8's scale puzzle: a fixed weight sits on the left pan and the
# right pan needs some subset of the items pulled from Moorlyn's debris
# piles to land exactly on "BALANCED". There's no numeric weight exposed,
# only a qualitative pointer position/overshoot message -- and with up to
# 16 known pile items (2^16 - 1 = 65,535 possible subsets), exhaustively
# trying every combination isn't practical. Known wording (confirmed
# live): "positioned to the left/right of the "BALANCED" indicator" and
# "There is too much weight on the <pan> pan." -- treated as balanced
# only when "BALANCED" appears WITHOUT either of those.
def guidebookquest_scale_balanced?(result)
  text = result.to_s
  text =~ /BALANCED/i && text !~ /to the (?:left|right) of|too much weight/i
end

# The game's own removal phrasing -- confirmed live -- only parses with
# the bare trailing noun ("get knife from scale"); the full two-word
# descriptor in that same slot ("get mildewed knife from scale") gets
# "Get what?" every time, even though that same full descriptor works
# fine for GET-from-pack and PUT-on-scale. Only removal needs this.
def guidebookquest_scale_removal_noun(item)
  item.to_s.split.last
end

# Tries MOORLYN_SCALE_SOLUTION directly when every item in it is
# available, instead of running the backtracking search. Confirmed
# live to balance once; if it doesn't this time (the pile pool differs
# run to run, so this isn't guaranteed), the scale is cleared back out
# before falling through to the real search.
def guidebookquest_apply_known_solution(items)
  return false unless MOORLYN_SCALE_SOLUTION.all? { |item| items.include?(item) }

  MOORLYN_SCALE_SOLUTION.each do |item|
    fput "get #{item}"
    sleep 0.3
    fput "put #{item} on scale"
    sleep 0.3
  end

  result = dothistimeout "look on scale", 5, /BALANCED|pan\./i
  if guidebookquest_scale_balanced?(result)
    echo "guidebookquest: scale balanced with the known solution (#{MOORLYN_SCALE_SOLUTION.join(', ')})."
    return true
  end

  echo "guidebookquest: known solution didn't balance this time -- clearing the scale and falling back to search."
  MOORLYN_SCALE_SOLUTION.each do |item|
    fput "get #{guidebookquest_scale_removal_noun(item)} from scale"
    sleep 0.3
    fput "stow right"
    sleep 0.3
  end
  false
end

# This is a real subset-sum search, not a one-shot greedy pass: items
# are added depth-first, and every item's weight is positive (it's a
# physical object), so once a branch overshoots into "too much weight"
# no amount of ADDING more items to it can ever come back down --
# that branch is pruned immediately (taken back off, not explored
# further) without needing to finish building out the full subset
# first. But unlike a pure greedy pass, failing to find a solution
# with an item included still backtracks and retries WITHOUT it
# (branch 2 below), rather than discarding it for good -- so a
# solution that needs an earlier item skipped in favor of a later
# one is still found. Worst case is still every subset (2^N), but the
# overshoot prune cuts off most branches long before they're complete.
def guidebookquest_balance_scale(items)
  if items.empty?
    echo "guidebookquest: no pile items were found to try on the scale."
    return false
  end

  return true if guidebookquest_apply_known_solution(items)

  on_scale = []
  solved = false

  remove_last = lambda do
    item = on_scale.pop
    fput "get #{guidebookquest_scale_removal_noun(item)} from scale"
    sleep 0.3
    fput "stow right"
    sleep 0.3
  end

  search = lambda do |index|
    next false if solved
    next false if index >= items.size

    item = items[index]

    # Branch 1: add this item, then either recurse deeper or prune.
    fput "get #{item}"
    sleep 0.3
    fput "put #{item} on scale"
    sleep 0.3
    on_scale << item

    result = dothistimeout "look on scale", 5, /BALANCED|pan\./i
    if guidebookquest_scale_balanced?(result)
      solved = true
      next true
    elsif result.to_s =~ /too much weight/i
      # Overshot -- this item can't be part of any solution built on
      # top of the current on_scale set; back it off and fall through
      # to branch 2 (skip it) without recursing deeper.
      remove_last.call
    else
      # Still light -- recurse to try adding more on top of it.
      next true if search.call(index + 1)
      # That subtree found nothing -- back this item off too before
      # falling through to branch 2.
      remove_last.call
    end

    # Branch 2: skip this item entirely, try the rest of the list.
    search.call(index + 1)
  end

  search.call(0)

  if solved
    echo "guidebookquest: scale balanced with #{on_scale.join(', ')}."
    true
  else
    echo "guidebookquest: no combination of #{items.join(', ')} balanced the scale."
    false
  end
end

# Chapter 8's pile-search-and-scale-balance sequence, shared between the
# normal INCOMPLETE branch (after it goes to Moorlyn and asks the ghoul)
# and the CHAPTER 8 RECOVERY branch below (QUEST INFO's "Thoroughly look
# around Moorlyn for a way to balance the scales." means the character
# is already there, so that entry point skips straight to this instead
# of going back to the entrance).
# Pulls whatever's in the current room's debris pile, if anything, and
# stows it. Confirmed live "get pile" is a no-op ("Get what?") in every
# room without a pile, and ("You already found something from this
# debris pile. There must be others around somewhere.") in a room
# that's already been searched.
def guidebookquest_moorlyn_search_room
  pile_result = dothistimeout "get pile", 3, /pull out|Get what\?|already found something/i
  return nil unless pile_result =~ /pull out (?:a|an) ([a-z][a-z' -]*)\./i

  item_name = Regexp.last_match(1).strip
  sleep 0.3
  fput "stow right"
  sleep 0.3
  item_name
end

# Confirmed live the hardcoded sw/east/nw walk recorded from one run
# didn't hold up on another -- "You can't go there" on several of the
# recorded steps, meaning Moorlyn's ring, like Gleyminn's river, is a
# different layout per character/run, not a fixed one worth hardcoding.
# What IS fixed: the scale's own room (Tree of Justice, u8083068) --
# confirmed across multiple runs. go2 still can't path anywhere in this
# zone at all, so this walks the ring one step at a time using whatever
# directions XMLData.room_exits_string actually lists at each room
# (same technique as guidebookquest_gleyminn_explore), always moving
# away from the direction just arrived from since every room here has
# exactly two exits -- there's no branching to get lost in, just a
# closed loop back to the start. Collects pile items and records the
# scale room's position in the walk as it's discovered, so the walk
# back to the scale afterward (handled by the caller) can go the short
# way regardless of which direction the items happened to be found in.
def guidebookquest_moorlyn_walk_ring(pile_items, max_steps: 20)
  moves = []
  scale_position = 0 if XMLData.room_id == 8083068
  item = guidebookquest_moorlyn_search_room
  pile_items << item if item && !pile_items.include?(item)

  max_steps.times do
    break if scale_position && MOORLYN_SCALE_SOLUTION.all? { |i| pile_items.include?(i) }

    exits = guidebookquest_room_exits
    came_from = moves.empty? ? nil : GLEYMINN_OPPOSITE_DIRECTION[moves.last]
    next_dir = exits.find { |d| d != came_from } || exits.first
    fput next_dir
    sleep 1
    moves << next_dir

    scale_position = moves.size if scale_position.nil? && XMLData.room_id == 8083068

    item = guidebookquest_moorlyn_search_room
    pile_items << item if item && !pile_items.include?(item)
  end

  [moves, scale_position]
end

def guidebookquest_moorlyn_resume
  # Resume safety: recover any pile items already carried over from an
  # earlier interrupted run (checked by actually looking inside worn/
  # held containers, not just GameObj's own top-level view -- see
  # guidebookquest_held_pile_items). If anything turns up, the pile
  # search is skipped entirely and this goes straight to the scale
  # instead of walking the whole swamp again for nothing.
  pile_items = guidebookquest_held_pile_items

  # Always walk the ring, even if items were already recovered above --
  # guidebookquest_moorlyn_walk_ring breaks as soon as both the items
  # are complete AND the scale room has been seen, so if the items are
  # already complete this just walks forward (collecting nothing new)
  # until it reaches the scale and stops there; it never backtracks or
  # risks oscillating in place the way a naive "step toward it" fallback
  # would.
  moves, scale_position = guidebookquest_moorlyn_walk_ring(pile_items)

  # Safety net: guidebookquest_moorlyn_walk_ring caps itself at 20 steps,
  # which covers every ring length confirmed live so far, but Moorlyn's
  # layout is now known to vary per character/run -- if a longer ring
  # ever means the scale room wasn't seen within that cap, keep walking
  # in fresh batches (each one resuming from wherever the last left off)
  # rather than let the ring math below divide-by-nil and crash the
  # script outright.
  attempts = 1
  while scale_position.nil? && attempts < 5
    more_moves, scale_position = guidebookquest_moorlyn_walk_ring(pile_items)
    moves += more_moves
    attempts += 1
  end

  unless scale_position
    echo "guidebookquest: couldn't find the scale's room after an extensive walk of Moorlyn -- stopping here instead of risking getting stuck."
    return
  end

  # Walk from wherever the loop above stopped to the scale's own room --
  # the shortest remaining stretch of the ring.
  stopped_at = moves.size
  remaining_to_scale = if stopped_at <= scale_position
                          moves[stopped_at...scale_position]
                        else
                          moves[stopped_at..-1] + moves[0...scale_position]
                        end
  remaining_to_scale.each do |dir|
    fput dir
    sleep 1
  end

  guidebookquest_balance_scale(pile_items)

  fput "get my guidebook"
  sleep 1
  fput "flip my guidebook"
  sleep 1
  fput "read my guidebook"
  sleep 1
end

# Chapter 12's hide-and-seek: each move/get needs its own pause -- firing
# fputs back-to-back outran movement and roundtime, so commands arrived
# before the room (or the hat) was ready and were silently dropped.
# Confirmed live: "get hat on/behind <target>" finds the hat (and puts it
# in hand) at exactly one spot along the whole route; once that happens
# you TAP it ("tap my felt hat") to send it flying back to the scarecrow
# and complete the chapter -- every other spot has no hat to get at all.
# Returns :found and stops early the moment that happens.
def guidebookquest_tap_hat_step(*commands)
  commands.each do |command|
    fput command
    waitrt?
    sleep 1
    if command =~ /\Aget hat\b/ && [GameObj.right_hand, GameObj.left_hand].compact.any? { |item| item.name =~ /hat/i }
      fput "tap my felt hat"
      waitrt?
      sleep 1
      return :found
    end
  end
  nil
end

GLEYMINN_OPPOSITE_DIRECTION = {
  "north" => "south", "south" => "north",
  "east" => "west", "west" => "east",
  "northeast" => "southwest", "southwest" => "northeast",
  "northwest" => "southeast", "southeast" => "northwest"
}.freeze

# Confirmed live: the Gleyminn Anni River (Chapter 11) is a real maze of
# fixed rooms reachable only by pushing a punted gondola in whichever
# directions that room actually lists -- go2 only knows the boatsman's
# own room, not anywhere past it ("No known route: I cannot find your
# current room (Gleyminn Anni River) on the map."), and a hardcoded
# direction list recorded on one run doesn't hold up ("You can't push a
# lantern-lit gondola that way." on several of the old recorded steps).
def guidebookquest_room_exits
  XMLData.room_exits_string.to_s.scan(/northeast|northwest|southeast|southwest|north|south|east|west/)
end

def guidebookquest_gleyminn_items_remaining?
  guidebookquest_quest_info_text.include?('remaining')
end

# Confirmed live: each pile item's room is fixed -- it's only WHICH
# items the boatsman currently wants that varies run to run, not where
# any given item actually is. A plain "search" at any room finds
# whatever item that room happens to have (auto-stowed, no "get"
# needed) and reports it by name, regardless of whether the boatsman
# currently wants it -- so every room below is worth visiting
# regardless of the current request, and QUEST INFO's "remaining" count
# decides when to stop. Add more entries here as further item/room
# pairs are confirmed live.
GLEYMINN_KNOWN_ITEM_ROOMS = {
  "fishing net" => "8083405",
  "lily pad" => "8083412",
  "fuzzy moss" => "8083419",
  "water reed" => "8083424",
  "leaf skeleton" => "8083422",
  "mournbloom" => "8083398"
}.freeze

def guidebookquest_gleyminn_search_room
  fput "search"
  waitrt?
  sleep 1
  fput "stow right"
end

# Visits every confirmed item room directly via go2 first -- much faster
# than the full maze DFS below when it's enough, and harmless to try
# even if the boatsman doesn't currently want any of these items, since
# a found item that isn't currently wanted just doesn't move QUEST
# INFO's "remaining" count (still picked up and stowed, costs nothing).
def guidebookquest_gleyminn_try_known_rooms
  GLEYMINN_KNOWN_ITEM_ROOMS.each_value do |room|
    Script.run("go2", go2_room("u#{room}"))
    sleep 1
    guidebookquest_gleyminn_search_room
    return true unless guidebookquest_gleyminn_items_remaining?
  end
  false
end

# Depth-first search: push an unexplored direction, recurse, and push the
# opposite direction right back if that branch dead-ends or loops back to
# an already-visited room -- until QUEST INFO no longer lists any of the
# boatsman's items as "remaining".
def guidebookquest_gleyminn_explore(visited = [])
  visited << XMLData.room_id
  guidebookquest_gleyminn_search_room
  return true unless guidebookquest_gleyminn_items_remaining?

  guidebookquest_room_exits.each do |dir|
    result = dothistimeout "push #{dir}", 5, /push a lantern-lit gondola|can't push a lantern-lit gondola/i
    next if result =~ /can't push/i
    waitrt?
    sleep 1

    if visited.include?(XMLData.room_id)
      fput "push #{GLEYMINN_OPPOSITE_DIRECTION[dir]}"
      waitrt?
      sleep 1
      next
    end

    return true if guidebookquest_gleyminn_explore(visited)

    fput "push #{GLEYMINN_OPPOSITE_DIRECTION[dir]}"
    waitrt?
    sleep 1
  end
  false
end

# Confirmed live: which room is correct for which item is NOT fixed --
# it varies run to run (one run placed the pendant where the toqua went
# on an earlier run, and vice versa), so there's no reliable per-item
# room worth hardcoding. What IS consistent: every room that's ever
# accepted a placement has "grave", "gravestone(s)", or "graveyard" in
# its own room description, and every room without that wording in its
# description always answers "This doesn't look like the right spot..."
# (and just drops the item on the ground) -- so only cycle through
# those rooms instead of the full graveyard. ("gravel" in a couple of
# other rooms' descriptions is coincidental and deliberately excluded.)
GRAVEYARD_TEMPORALIS_GRAVE_ROOMS = %w[
  8083322 8083323 8083325 8083326 8083327 8083331 8083334 8083337 8083338
  8083339 8083340 8083341 8083343 8083346 8083348 8083349 8083351 8083352
  8083354 8083357
].freeze

# Confirmed live wording for the graveyard item-placement puzzle: a wrong
# spot either rejects outright ("This doesn't look like the right spot
# to place...", "You decide not to disturb this particular grave, as it
# looks a bit fresh.") or just drops the item on the ground, while the
# correct spot has an NPC spirit immediately take it ("With the utmost
# care and respect, you place a ... beside ..."). The bare noun is
# ambiguous for at least one of these items -- confirmed live "get sash"
# picked up the WRONG item ("You already have that." / "You are not
# holding that." on the follow-up "place sash", since this character
# also carries an unrelated "pewter-pinned auroralaen sash") -- so this
# always uses a two-word descriptor, never the bare noun.
def guidebookquest_graveyard_try_place(descriptor)
  fput "get #{descriptor}"
  sleep 0.3
  result = dothistimeout "place #{descriptor}", 5, /With the utmost care and respect|doesn't look like the right spot|decide not to disturb/i
  result =~ /With the utmost care and respect/i
end

def guidebookquest_graveyard_place_item(descriptor)
  GRAVEYARD_TEMPORALIS_GRAVE_ROOMS.each do |room|
    Script.run("go2", go2_room("u#{room}"))
    sleep 1
    return true if guidebookquest_graveyard_try_place(descriptor)
  end
  echo "guidebookquest: couldn't find a resting spot for the #{descriptor} in any known grave/gravestone room -- leaving it for a manual PLACE."
  false
end

# Confirmed live from a cold start: a character whose hands are already
# full (e.g. a drawn weapon and shield) can't just "get guidebook" --
# "Both of your hands are full.  You'll need to put something away
# before you can pick anything up." -- stow everything and retry.
def guidebookquest_ensure_guidebook_in_hand
  return if [GameObj.right_hand, GameObj.left_hand].compact.any? { |item| item.name =~ /guidebook/i }

  result = dothistimeout "get guidebook", 5, /which attunes to you|Both of your hands are full|Get what\?/i
  return unless result =~ /Both of your hands are full/i

  fput "stow all"
  sleep 1
  fput "get guidebook"
  sleep 1
end

# Maps a QUEST INFO objective's own wording (left) to the matching
# guidebook INCOMPLETE trigger text (right, copy-pasted verbatim from
# that chapter's own "elsif line =~ /.../ " in guidebookquest_dispatch
# below) -- once an objective is mapped, that chapter's existing
# dispatch logic handles it regardless of whether the script arrived
# there via the book or via QUEST INFO. Entries below are pre-filled
# with each chapter's trigger_line; only the left-hand placeholder
# regex (/FILL_IN_CHAPTER_N/) needs replacing once that chapter's real
# QUEST INFO wording is confirmed live -- leave a placeholder as-is
# (it can never accidentally match real game text) for any chapter not
# yet confirmed.
QUEST_INFO_TRIGGER_MAP = {
  # Chapter 1 -- Welcome to the Unliving (Town Hall / get the guidebook).
  # Low value to map -- this step happens before QUEST INFO is even
  # meaningful -- but left in for completeness.
  /Travel to Town Hall and speak with the skeletal town hall clerk/i => "You can't do that",
  # Chapter 2 -- Evermore Hollow Town Proper (merchant visits)
  /FILL_IN_CHAPTER_2/i => "Seek out various merchants in Evermore Hollow and speak with them about a variety of topics",
  # Chapter 3 -- Evermore Hollow Hotel (bellhop/paintings)
  /Visit the bellhop at the Evermore Hotel/i => "The bellhop may have information that you want to hear",
  # Chapter 3 recovery -- the magnifying glass has already been handed
  # out at this point, so this skips straight to the painting-waving
  # (see the CHAPTER 3 RECOVERY branch in guidebookquest_dispatch below)
  # instead of re-running the whole bellhop hunt for a second glass.
  /Investigate the three paintings in the Evermore Hotel/i => "WAVE GLASS AT PAINTINGS",
  # Chapter 4 -- Fate's Holding (skeleton convict/wine)
  /Look for the skeleton convict in Fate's Holding/i => "Be on the lookout for a skeleton convict somewhere in Fate",
  # Chapter 5 -- Innocent's End (stuffed bear)
  /Visit an eyepatch-clad stuffed toy bear in Innocent's End/i => "An unlikely assistant you will find in Innocent",
  # Chapter 6 -- Vigil's End (legionnaire/monocle)
  /Speak to the legionnaire in Vigil's End to see if she has any quests she needs completed/i => "Speak with the legionnaire in Vigil's End",
  # Chapter 6 recovery -- confirmed live the book can keep flipping
  # forward through every later chapter while this never gets resolved,
  # if the final "give legionnaire" silently failed earlier (see the
  # CHAPTER 6 RECOVERY branch in guidebookquest_dispatch below). Doesn't
  # re-run the whole hunt/placement sequence -- just finishes handing in
  # the monocle.
  /Return the monocle to the legionnaire in Vigil's End/i => "RETURN MONOCLE TO LEGIONNAIRE",
  # Chapter 7 -- snowman/paper
  /Find a friendly snowman in Winterbriar and ask if it has any quests for you./i => 'Find a "snowkin", of sorts, with a welcoming personality',
  # Chapter 8 -- Moorlyn (ghoul/scale)
  /Head to Moorlyn and find the wandering ghoul to ask if he needs any assistance with a quest./i => "Head to Moorlyn and search for the truth",
  # Chapter 8, further along -- already in the swamp with the ghoul
  # asked, just need the pile search and scale (see the CHAPTER 8
  # RECOVERY branch in guidebookquest_dispatch below).
  /Thoroughly look around Moorlyn for a way to balance the scales/i => "MOORLYN SCALE RESUME",
  # Chapter 9 -- Graveyard Temporalis (spirit/lily)
  /Find the spirit in the Graveyard Temporalis./i => "Graves and tombstones in graveyards hold information, and the ones in the Graveyard Temporalis are no exception",
  # Chapter 10 -- Bittermere Woods (wandering spirit/lily)
  /Find the luminous cyan-hued spirit in Bittermere Woods and ask if she has any quests for you./i => "This quest begins with a luminous cyan-hued spirit in Bittermere Woods",
  # Chapter 11 -- Gleyminn Anni River (boatsman)
  /Ask the boatsman in the Gleyminn Anni River area if he has any quests for you.|The boatsman requested that you find the following items/i => "This quest begins with a hunchbacked boatsman in the Gleyminn Anni River area",
  # Chapter 12 -- Ember Ridge Orchard (hide-and-seek hat hunt)
  /Find the scarecrow in Ember Ridge Orchard.|Thoroughly look around Ember Ridge Orchard for the hat./i => "Don't let it scare you away",
  # Chapter 13 -- Ember Ridge Orchard (pumpkin carving/PLACE JACK)
  /Visit the pumpkin patch in Ember Ridge Orchard./i => "You can find all you need in Ember Ridge Orchard",
  # Chapter 14 -- Epilogue/merchant listing
  /FILL_IN_CHAPTER_14/i => "This quest is incomplete"
}.freeze

# QUEST INFO is checked FIRST, ahead of the guidebook, both at startup
# and after every chapter completion -- confirmed live its wording for
# the same step doesn't always match the guidebook's own text, and a
# repeat questor (quest completed in a prior year) finds every chapter
# of the guidebook reads back as already complete, sometimes cascading
# through several already-complete pages in a row via the generic "You
# completed this chapter" trigger with no useful INCOMPLETE text at all.
# QUEST_INFO_TRIGGER_MAP maps a QUEST INFO objective's own wording to
# the guidebook INCOMPLETE trigger text for the SAME chapter, so once an
# objective is mapped here, that chapter's existing dispatch logic
# handles it either way the script arrives there -- add more entries as
# further QUEST INFO wording gets confirmed live. Only once nothing here
# matches does the caller fall back to reading the book and letting its
# own per-chapter triggers take it from there.
def guidebookquest_quest_info_redirect
  text = guidebookquest_quest_info_text
  return false if text.empty?

  objective = guidebookquest_quest_objective(text)
  QUEST_INFO_TRIGGER_MAP.each do |regex, trigger_line|
    next unless objective =~ regex
    echo "guidebookquest: QUEST INFO objective -- #{objective} -- resuming at the matching chapter."
    guidebookquest_dispatch(trigger_line)
    return true
  end
  false
end

def guidebookquest_dispatch(line)
  ##############################################################################
  # CHAPTER 1 INCOMPLETE
  if line =~ /You can't do that/
    Script.run("go2", go2_room("u8084004"))
    guidebookquest_ensure_guidebook_in_hand
    fput "open my guidebook"
    fput "read my guidebook"
  # CHAPTERS 1-12 COMPLETED (generic)
  #
  # Each chapter used to be matched on its own unique bit of completed
  # flavor text (e.g. Chapter 1's was literally "By now you have noticed
  # our twilight skies and cobbled streets"). That's risky: some of those
  # lines read exactly like an ordinary room description (Chapter 8's old
  # trigger, "Positioned on the outskirts of Bittermere Woods, Moorlyn",
  # is basically indistinguishable from one), so the script could flip
  # the guidebook forward from a line that had nothing to do with the
  # chapter actually being finished. "You completed this chapter." is
  # the one confirmed-live phrase that's never ambiguous -- it's the
  # game's own confirmation, not narrative text -- so that's the only
  # thing allowed to trigger a flip now. (Chapter 13's completion uses
  # its own separate trigger below, since it also needs to run
  # guidebookquest_discard_pile_items.)
  elsif line =~ /You completed this chapter/
    guidebookquest_log_quest_info
    fput "flip my guidebook"
    sleep 1
    # Always check QUEST INFO first now, rather than trusting whatever
    # page the book landed on: confirmed live its wording can differ
    # from the guidebook's own text for the same step, and for a repeat
    # questor the book can cascade through several already-complete
    # pages in a row with no useful INCOMPLETE text at all. A known
    # QUEST INFO objective dispatches straight to the matching chapter
    # (see QUEST_INFO_TRIGGER_MAP/guidebookquest_quest_info_redirect) --
    # only fall back to reading the book, and letting its own per-
    # chapter triggers handle whatever it shows, when QUEST INFO doesn't
    # match anything mapped yet.
    unless guidebookquest_quest_info_redirect
      fput "read my guidebook"
      sleep 1
    end
  ##############################################################################
  # CHAPTER 2 INCOMPLETE
  elsif line =~ /Seek out various merchants in Evermore Hollow and speak with them about a variety of topics/
    fput "stow my guidebook"
    Script.run("go2", go2_room("u8084052"))
    fput "ask witch about quest"
    Script.run("go2", go2_room("u8084066"))
    fput "ask spectre about quest"
    Script.run("go2", go2_room("u8084071"))
    fput "ask zombie about quest"
    Script.run("go2", go2_room("u8084056"))
    fput "ask cailleach about quest"
    Script.run("go2", go2_room("u8084061"))
    fput "ask korrigan about quest"
    Script.run("go2", go2_room("u8084062"))
    fput "ask korrigan about quest"
    Script.run("go2", go2_room("u8084076"))
    fput "ask skeleton about quest"
    Script.run("go2", go2_room("u8084077"))
    fput "ask skeleton about quest"
    fput "get my guidebook"
    fput "read my guidebook"
  ##############################################################################
  # CHAPTER 3 INCOMPLETE
  elsif line =~ /The bellhop may have information that you want to hear/
    fput "stow my guidebook"
    # The bellhop wanders the hotel rather than sitting in a fixed room,
    # so this walks the hotel's own rooms (every uid seen on a full
    # circuit: Foyer, Hallway, Grand Hall, Sitting Room, Lounge,
    # Restaurant, Solarium, Platform, Bar, Dressing Room, the upstairs
    # Hallway, Basement, Wine Cellar, Mud Baths, Stair Base) checking for
    # it at each stop, instead of the old fixed list of (wrong) room ids
    # that assumed it was in all 14 at once. Used twice below: once to
    # find it for the initial ask (which hands over a magnifying glass),
    # once more afterward to hand the glass back in, since it may have
    # wandered off in between.
    bellhop_hotel_rooms = %w[8083011 8087101 8087102 8087163 8087124 8087149 8087128
                              8087162 8087126 8087103 8087125 8087160 8087165 8087166
                              8087167 8087161]
    find_bellhop = lambda do
      bellhop_hotel_rooms.each do |room|
        Script.run("go2", go2_room("u#{room}"))
        return true if GameObj.npcs.any? { |npc| npc.name =~ /bellhop/i }
      end
      false
    end

    if find_bellhop.call
      fput "ask bellhop about quest"
    else
      echo "guidebookquest: bellhop not found on a full circuit of the hotel -- Chapter 3's room list may need another pass added."
    end

    # Three rooms confirmed live to have a paintable painting: the
    # bellhop's own upstairs Hallway, the Stair Base, and the Lounge.
    # "wave glass at painting" is harmless to repeat ("You already
    # investigated that painting."), so revisiting one already done from
    # an earlier partial run isn't a problem.
    painting_rooms = %w[8087160 8087161 8087149]
    painting_rooms.each do |room|
      Script.run("go2", go2_room("u#{room}"))
      fput "wave glass at painting"
    end

    if find_bellhop.call
      fput "get my glass" unless GameObj.right_hand && GameObj.right_hand.noun =~ /glass/i
      fput "give bellhop"
    else
      echo "guidebookquest: bellhop not found for the magnifying glass hand-in."
    end

    fput "stow all"
    fput "get my guidebook"
    fput "read my guidebook"
  ##############################################################################
  # CHAPTER 3 RECOVERY (QUEST INFO only -- see QUEST_INFO_TRIGGER_MAP)
  #
  # QUEST INFO's "Investigate the three paintings..." objective means
  # the magnifying glass has already been handed out -- re-running the
  # full Chapter 3 branch above would send the script hunting for the
  # bellhop to ask for a SECOND glass it doesn't need. This skips
  # straight to waving it at the three known painting rooms and handing
  # it back in.
  elsif line == "WAVE GLASS AT PAINTINGS"
    fput "stow my guidebook"
    bellhop_hotel_rooms = %w[8083011 8087101 8087102 8087163 8087124 8087149 8087128
                              8087162 8087126 8087103 8087125 8087160 8087165 8087166
                              8087167 8087161]
    find_bellhop = lambda do
      bellhop_hotel_rooms.each do |room|
        Script.run("go2", go2_room("u#{room}"))
        return true if GameObj.npcs.any? { |npc| npc.name =~ /bellhop/i }
      end
      false
    end

    painting_rooms = %w[8087160 8087161 8087149]
    painting_rooms.each do |room|
      Script.run("go2", go2_room("u#{room}"))
      fput "wave glass at painting"
    end

    if find_bellhop.call
      fput "get my glass" unless GameObj.right_hand && GameObj.right_hand.noun =~ /glass/i
      fput "give bellhop"
    else
      echo "guidebookquest: bellhop not found for the magnifying glass hand-in."
    end

    fput "stow all"
    fput "get my guidebook"
    fput "read my guidebook"
  ##############################################################################
  # CHAPTER 4 INCOMPLETE
  elsif line =~ /Be on the lookout for a skeleton convict somewhere in Fate|Look for the skeleton convict in Fate\'s Holding/
    fput "stow my guidebook"
    sleep 1
    # Fate's Holding, The Cell -- the skeleton convict asks for wine from
    # "the witch" (the gjenganger next door) in exchange for pages.
    Script.run("go2", go2_room("u8083134"))
    fput "ask skeleton about quest"
    sleep 1
    # Fate's Holding, The Cauldron -- asking the gjenganger about drink
    # hands over a corked bottle of wine directly.
    Script.run("go2", go2_room("u8083133"))
    fput "ask gjeng about drink"
    sleep 1
    Script.run("go2", go2_room("u8083134"))
    # Giving the wine prompts a 30-second confirmation window ("please do
    # so again in the next 30 seconds") before the skeleton actually
    # takes it and drinks it -- the second give below satisfies that.
    # He ends up too distracted to hand over the promised pages either
    # way; this step is still "the chapter's done" per the guidebook, not
    # a failure to recover from.
    fput "give skeleton"
    sleep 1
    fput "give skeleton"
    sleep 1
    fput "get my guidebook"
    sleep 1
    # No blind flip here -- let the generic "You completed this chapter"
    # trigger decide whether this actually finished; flipping
    # unconditionally risked skipping the chapter even when it hadn't
    # (confirmed live elsewhere: Chapter 10's copy of this same pattern
    # flipped straight past an unfinished chapter into the next one).
    fput "read my guidebook"
    sleep 1
  ##############################################################################
  # CHAPTER 5 INCOMPLETE
  elsif line =~ /An unlikely assistant you will find in Innocent|Visit an eyepatch\-clad stuffed toy bear in Innocent\'s End and ask about the quest/
    fput "stow my guidebook"
    sleep 1
    # Innocent's End, The Carefree -- Stuffy (the stuffed toy bear) hands
    # out this chapter's three tasks one at a time, each handing the
    # needed item straight into your right hand (no separate GET needed).
    # Only one "ask bear about quest" per visit is needed; the second ask
    # in the old version just got "I don't need any help right now."
    bear_room = "u8083123"
    Script.run("go2", go2_room(bear_room))
    fput "ask bear about quest"
    sleep 1

    # Task 1: plant the seeds by the otter-shaped fountain in The
    # Zoophilist.
    Script.run("go2", go2_room("u8083119"))
    fput "bury seeds"
    waitrt?
    Script.run("go2", go2_room(bear_room))
    fput "ask bear about quest"
    sleep 1

    # Task 2: wave the flag at the ship-shaped buildings in The
    # Adventurers to get the cannon-firing children to cease fire.
    # Waving it anywhere else along the way is a harmless no-op ("You
    # wave your white flag around.") -- only this room actually
    # completes the task.
    Script.run("go2", go2_room("u8083122"))
    fput "wave flag"
    waitrt?
    Script.run("go2", go2_room(bear_room))
    fput "ask bear about quest"
    sleep 1

    # Task 3: wave the toy boat in The Destroyed to coax the hiding
    # children out.
    Script.run("go2", go2_room("u8083116"))
    fput "wave my boat"
    waitrt?

    fput "get my guidebook"
    sleep 1
    fput "read my guidebook"
    sleep 1
  ##############################################################################
  # CHAPTER 6 INCOMPLETE
  elsif line =~ /Speak with the legionnaire in Vigil\'s End|Speak to the legionnaire in Vigil\'s End to see if she has any quests she needs completed/
    fput "stow my guidebook"
    sleep 1
    # Vigil's End, Armistice Avenue -- the legionnaire hands over a
    # mithril-edged monocle directly; only one ask is needed.
    Script.run("go2", go2_room("u8083102"))
    fput "ask legion about quest"
    sleep 1

    # "Observe mithril monocle" at each of these five rooms finds one
    # specific hidden item (confirmed live -- a much larger set of rooms
    # were tried along the way and all came back "you don't see anything
    # of note," these five are the only ones that actually have
    # something): a bloodstained torn linen toqua, a fire-scorched
    # leather headband, a sodden elesine sash, a cracked agate pony
    # pendant, and a fractured vultite armband. The game itself announces
    # "You found all the items" after the fifth.
    monocle_item_rooms = %w[8083101 8083098 8083089 8083096 8083097]
    monocle_item_rooms.each do |room|
      Script.run("go2", go2_room("u#{room}"))
      fput "observe mithril monocle"
      sleep 1
      fput "stow left"
      sleep 1
    end

    # Confirmed live: each item has its own correct grave/marker in the
    # Graveyard Temporalis -- a spirit NPC shows up and takes it the
    # moment it's placed in the right spot (see
    # GRAVEYARD_TEMPORALIS_GRAVE_ROOMS above for why which room is which
    # isn't hardcoded). Two-word descriptors, not bare nouns -- "sash"
    # alone is ambiguous with an unrelated item this character also
    # carries.
    ["vultite armband", "elesine sash", "pony pendant", "linen toqua", "leather headband"].each do |descriptor|
      guidebookquest_graveyard_place_item(descriptor)
    end

    Script.run("go2", go2_room("u8083102"))
    sleep 1
    # Confirmed live: without this, "give legionnaire" can silently fail
    # or give the wrong item if the monocle got stowed away at some
    # point during the graveyard placement loop above (which repeatedly
    # swaps hands for other items) -- make sure it's actually in hand
    # first.
    fput "get my mithril monocle"
    sleep 1
    fput "give legionnaire"
    fput "get my guidebook"
    sleep 1
    fput "read my guidebook"
    sleep 1
  ##############################################################################
  # CHAPTER 6 RECOVERY (QUEST INFO only -- see QUEST_INFO_TRIGGER_MAP)
  #
  # Confirmed live: a character can end up with the guidebook already
  # flipped all the way to Chapter 14 while QUEST INFO still says
  # "Return the monocle to the legionnaire in Vigil's End." -- the
  # monocle hunt and graveyard placements from Chapter 6 above had
  # already been done, but the final "give legionnaire" silently failed
  # (the monocle wasn't in hand -- see the fix above), and the book kept
  # right on advancing through every later chapter regardless, with
  # nothing in their own INCOMPLETE text ever pointing back at this.
  # Re-running the whole Chapter 6 sequence from scratch would be wrong
  # at that point (the hunt/placements are already done); this just
  # finishes the one actual remaining step.
  elsif line == "RETURN MONOCLE TO LEGIONNAIRE"
    Script.run("go2", go2_room("u8083102"))
    sleep 1
    fput "get my mithril monocle"
    sleep 1
    fput "give legionnaire"
    sleep 1
  ##############################################################################
  # CHAPTER 7 INCOMPLETE
  elsif line =~ /Find a "snowkin", of sorts, with a welcoming personality/
    fput "stow my guidebook"
    sleep 1
    # If a snow white piece of paper is already on hand (hands or
    # inventory -- e.g. the script got interrupted and restarted
    # mid-chapter), skip dropping it and asking the snowman for a fresh
    # one; just re-whisper every answer onto the one we've already got
    # and go straight to the hand-in.
    already_have_paper = [GameObj.right_hand, GameObj.left_hand, *Array(GameObj.inv)]
                          .compact.any? { |item| item.name.to_s =~ /white paper/i || item.noun.to_s =~ /paper/i }

    if already_have_paper
      Script.run("go2", go2_room("u8083070"))
    else
      fput "drop my white paper"
      sleep 1
      Script.run("go2", go2_room("u8083070"))
      fput "ask snowman about quest"
      sleep 1
    end
    fput "whisper my white paper 1 snowman"
    sleep 1
    fput "whisper my white paper 2 garden of shadows"
    sleep 1
    fput "whisper my white paper 3 ten"
    sleep 1
    fput "whisper my white paper 4 ianthine"
    sleep 1
    fput "whisper my white paper 5 unicorn"
    sleep 1
    fput "whisper my white paper 6 aconite"
    sleep 1
    fput "give snowman"
    fput "get my guidebook"
    sleep 1
    fput "read my guidebook"
    sleep 1
  ##############################################################################
  # CHAPTER 8 INCOMPLETE
  elsif line =~ /Head to Moorlyn and search for the truth/
    fput "stow my guidebook"
    sleep 1
    Script.run("go2", go2_room("u8083054"))
    fput "ask ghoul about quest"
    sleep 1
    guidebookquest_moorlyn_resume
  ##############################################################################
  # CHAPTER 8 RECOVERY (QUEST INFO only -- see QUEST_INFO_TRIGGER_MAP)
  #
  # QUEST INFO's "Thoroughly look around Moorlyn for a way to balance
  # the scales." means the character is already in the swamp -- skips
  # the entrance/go2/ask-ghoul bootstrap above and goes straight to the
  # pile search and scale balancing.
  elsif line == "MOORLYN SCALE RESUME"
    guidebookquest_moorlyn_resume
  ##############################################################################
  # CHAPTER 9 INCOMPLETE
  elsif line =~ /Graves and tombstones in graveyards hold information, and the ones in the Graveyard Temporalis are no exception/
    fput "tell spirit to leave" if Spell[218].active?
    fput "stow my guidebook"
    sleep 1
    # Confirmed live: just the one spirit, in the Graveyard Temporalis
    # itself (RN 8083327) -- the earlier 5-room LN loop was unconfirmed
    # guesswork and wrong.
    Script.run("go2", go2_room("u8083327"))
    fput "ask spirit about quest"
    sleep 1

    lily_found = false
    5.times do
      break if lily_found

      # Confirmed live command is "forage black lily" (the item that comes
      # back is "a matte black vampire lily").
      lily = dothistimeout "forage black lily", 5, /You forage around but find nothing of interest|You forage briefly and manage to find a matte black vampire lily/
      if lily =~ /You forage briefly and manage to find a matte black vampire lily/
        lily_found = true
        waitrt?
      end
    end
    unless lily_found
      fput "forage black lily"
      waitrt?
    end

    fput "get my black lily"
    sleep 0.3
    # Confirmed live: a plain "give spirit" with the guidebook in the
    # wrong hand gives the GUIDEBOOK instead ("This is not quite what I
    # desire.") -- make sure the lily is in hand, not the book, first.
    if GameObj.right_hand && GameObj.right_hand.noun !~ /lily/i
      fput "swap"
      sleep 0.3
    end
    fput "give spirit"
    sleep 1
    fput "get my guidebook"
    sleep 1
    # No blind flip here -- see the note on this same removed pattern in
    # Chapter 4; let the generic "You completed this chapter" trigger
    # decide whether this actually finished.
    fput "read my guidebook"
    sleep 1
  ##############################################################################
  # CHAPTER 10 INCOMPLETE
  elsif line =~ /This quest begins with a luminous cyan-hued spirit in Bittermere Woods/
    fput "tell spirit to leave" if Spell[218].active?
    fput "stow my guidebook"
    sleep 1
    # Confirmed live: the spirit wanders Bittermere Woods rather than
    # sitting still at Convergence (RN 8083262, where it was first seen)
    # -- walk the zone's known rooms checking for it, same pattern as
    # Chapter 3's wandering bellhop, instead of assuming it's always at
    # one fixed spot.
    bittermere_spirit_rooms = %w[8083262 8083253 8083250 8083247 8083256 8083259
                                  8083258 8083255 8083252 8083248 8083251 8083268
                                  8083271 8083275 8083278 8083283 8083276 8083267
                                  8083270 8083273 8083277 8083279 8083272]
    find_spirit = lambda do
      bittermere_spirit_rooms.each do |room|
        Script.run("go2", go2_room("u#{room}"))
        return true if GameObj.npcs.any? { |npc| npc.name =~ /spirit/i }
      end
      false
    end

    if find_spirit.call
      fput "ask spirit about quest"
      sleep 1
    end

    # The azure calla lily room itself is fixed (RN 8083275) regardless
    # of where the spirit was found; asking it tells you to drink from
    # the magic, and a plain "get lily" there does the drinking itself
    # (no separate "drink lily" needed -- that failed live with "But you
    # are not holding an oversized azure calla lily.").
    Script.run("go2", go2_room("u8083275"))
    fput "get lily"
    sleep 1
    fput "get my guidebook"
    sleep 1
    # No blind flip here -- this was exactly the bug confirmed live: it
    # flipped straight past Chapter 10 into Chapter 11 even when "get
    # lily" hadn't actually finished the chapter. Let the generic "You
    # completed this chapter" trigger decide.
    fput "read my guidebook"
    sleep 1
  ##############################################################################
  # CHAPTER 11 INCOMPLETE
  elsif line =~ /This quest begins with a hunchbacked boatsman in the Gleyminn Anni River area/
    fput "stow my guidebook"
    sleep 1
    Script.run("go2", go2_room("u8083384"))
    fput "ask boatsman about quest"
    sleep 1
    fput "ask boatsman about quest"
    sleep 1

    # Confirmed live: the sailcloth sack puzzle (and its "observe") is
    # gone. The river itself is a real maze of fixed rooms reachable only
    # by pushing the gondola -- no hardcoded direction list survives a
    # replay. Each item's own room IS fixed, though (only which items the
    # boatsman currently wants varies), so try the growing list of known
    # item rooms directly first (GLEYMINN_KNOWN_ITEM_ROOMS) before falling
    # back to the full depth-first search for anything still missing.
    unless guidebookquest_gleyminn_try_known_rooms
      Script.run("go2", go2_room("u8083384"))
      sleep 1
      guidebookquest_gleyminn_explore
    end

    Script.run("go2", go2_room("u8083384"))
    waitrt?
    sleep 1
    Script.run("go2", go2_room("u8083384"))
    waitrt?
    sleep 1
    fput "give boatsman"
    fput "get my guidebook"
    sleep 1
    fput "read my guidebook"
    sleep 1
  ##############################################################################
  # CHAPTER 12 INCOMPLETE
  elsif line =~ /Don't let it scare you away/
    fput "stow my guidebook"
    sleep 1
    Script.run("go2", go2_room("u8083174"))
    sleep 1
    # The hide-and-seek scavenger hunt: a fixed walk-and-search route
    # recorded for this layout. Like Chapter 11's sailcloth sack, this
    # doesn't adapt to a reshuffled layout -- fall back to exploring and
    # searching by hand if it stops matching. The hat only turns up at
    # one spot; guidebookquest_tap_hat_step detects it, taps it, and
    # returns :found, so the route stops as soon as that happens instead
    # of walking the rest of it pointlessly.
    hat_hunt_route = [
      ["tap hat on scarecrow", "get hat on sign", "get hat behind sign"],
      ["nw", "get hat", "get hat on bench"],
      ["se", "sw", "get hat", "get hat on patch"],
      ["sw", "get hat", "get hat on table", "get hat on bench", "get hat on gazebo"],
      ["ne", "ne", "ne", "get hat", "get hat on leaves"],
      ["ne", "get hat"],
      ["nw", "get hat", "get hat on bench"],
      ["se", "ne", "get hat", "get hat on sign", "get hat on basket"],
      ["e", "get hat"],
      ["e", "get hat"],
      ["s", "get hat"],
      ["s", "get hat"],
      ["n", "n", "e", "get hat"],
      ["e", "get hat"],
      ["se", "get hat"],
      ["ne", "get hat"],
      ["sw", "se", "get hat"],
      ["se", "get hat"],
      ["ne", "get hat"],
      ["sw", "se", "get hat"],
      ["se", "get hat"]
    ]
    hat_hunt_route.each do |commands|
      break if guidebookquest_tap_hat_step(*commands) == :found
    end

    fput "get my guidebook"
    sleep 1
    fput "read my guidebook"
    sleep 1
  ##############################################################################
  # CHAPTER 13 INCOMPLETE
  elsif line =~ /You can find all you need in Ember Ridge Orchard/
    fput "stow my guidebook"
    sleep 1
    # Confirmed live: the pumpkin patch is u8083177 ("go patch" then
    # "get pump"); the carving table/tool/votive are at the Gazebo,
    # u8083179. You must be kneeling before "carve pump" works
    # ("It'd be a lot easier to carve your bright orange pumpkin if you
    # were sitting down.") -- get the tool first, then kneel, then carve.
    # The carved jack-o'-lantern ends up straight in hand; no separate
    # "get jack" is needed or possible ("I could not find what you were
    # referring to.").
    Script.run("go2", go2_room("u8083177"))
    sleep 1
    fput "go patch"
    sleep 1
    fput "get pump"
    sleep 1

    Script.run("go2", go2_room("u8083179"))
    sleep 1
    fput "get tool"
    sleep 1
    fput "kneel"
    sleep 1
    fput "carve pump"
    waitrt?
    fput "stand"
    sleep 1
    fput "get votive"
    sleep 1

    Script.run("go2", go2_room("exit"))
    sleep 1

    # PLACE JACK fails in a crowded spot ("...already a couple pumpkins
    # on display, you decide to look for a less crowded area...") and
    # succeeds in a quieter one -- confirmed live that the exit landing
    # room is crowded but one room southeast of it isn't. Try the landing
    # spot first, then that one fallback, before giving up to a manual
    # pause (layout may differ from this recorded case).
    placed = false
    ["", "se"].each do |dir|
      unless dir.empty?
        fput dir
        sleep 1
      end
      result = dothistimeout "place jack", 5, /suitable place to display|less crowded area/i
      if result =~ /suitable place to display/i
        placed = true
        break
      end
    end

    unless placed
      echo "XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX"
      echo "  Go to any room in Evermore Hollow and attempt to  "
      echo "                     PLACE JACK                     "
      echo "    Once you do this, ;unpause all this script.     "
      echo "XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX"
      pause_script "guidebookquest"
    end

    fput "put votive in jack"
    sleep 1
    fput "light votive"
    sleep 1
    fput "light votive"
    sleep 1
    waitrt?
    # The game doesn't auto-claim this -- confirmed live: "Welcome to
    # Evermore Hollow is finished!  Use QUEST COMPLETE to claim rewards."
    # Confirmed live: waiting for the main dispatch loop to see
    # "You have completed..." as its own later line wasn't reliable --
    # the "get"/"read" fputs below can end up advancing the guidebook
    # into a brand new chapter (even showing that NEW chapter's own
    # "You completed this chapter.") before the main loop ever gets
    # around to processing the quest-complete confirmation at all, so
    # the CHAPTER 13 COMPLETED exit below never fired. Capturing the
    # response to "quest complete" directly here and exiting immediately
    # removes that race entirely instead of trusting it'll turn up later.
    result = dothistimeout "quest complete", 5, /You have completed|Welcome to Evermore Hollow is finished|not currently|no active/i
    if result =~ /You have completed/i
      guidebookquest_discard_pile_items
      echo "guidebookquest: quest complete -- stopping."
      exit
    end

    fput "get my guidebook"
    sleep 1
    fput "read my guidebook"
    sleep 1
  # CHAPTER 13 COMPLETED
  elsif line =~ /You completed this quest/ || line =~ /You have completed #{Regexp.escape(QUEST_NAME)}/i
    guidebookquest_log_quest_info
    fput "flip my guidebook"
    fput "read my guidebook"
    guidebookquest_discard_pile_items
    echo "guidebookquest: quest complete -- stopping."
    exit
  ##############################################################################
  # CHAPTER 14 INCOMPLETE
  elsif line =~ /This quest is incomplete/
    fput "flip my guidebook"
    sleep 1
    fput "read my guidebook"
    sleep 1
  # CHAPTER 14 COMPLETED
  elsif line =~ /MERCHANT NAME/
    guidebookquest_log_quest_info
    fput "flip my guidebook"
    fput "read my guidebook"
  end
end

guidebookquest_ensure_guidebook_in_hand
unless guidebookquest_quest_info_redirect
  # Confirmed live: reading a freshly-gotten guidebook before opening it
  # just shows the title page ("*-* Guidebook To The Unliving *-*") with
  # no chapter text -- "open my guidebook" first is required.
  fput "open my guidebook"
  sleep 0.3
  fput "read my guidebook"
  sleep 1
end

while (line = get)
  guidebookquest_dispatch(line)
end
